Optical Music Recognition has traditionally been addressed one notation at a time, with separate systems for common Western music, Mensural sources and chant. Our work at the University of Alicante follows a different path, applying a single approach to neumatic, square, mensural and modern staff notation alike. Two ideas underpin it. First, identifying symbols is not equivalent to reading music. The duration of a mensural note depends on its context, and the assignment of syllables to notes depends on how the text is set. A system must therefore incorporate musical knowledge. Second, OMR output always requires human verification. The effort of the person reviewing and correcting it is the main cost of the process, and the tools must be designed around that person as much as around the models. The talk illustrates both ideas with examples from the MuRET ecosystem and discusses what has proved effective and where difficulties remain.
